<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Napirisha</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Napirisha, 'great god', was one of the chief deities of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elam">Elam</a> in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, worshipped with the goddess Kiririsha and the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inshushinak">Inshushinak</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susa">Susa</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-elamite-period">Middle Elamite period</a> he headed the state pantheon, and the king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/untash-napirisha">Untash-Napirisha</a>, whose name honours him, dedicated the great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziggurat">ziggurat</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chogha-zanbil">Chogha Zanbil</a> to him and Inshushinak in the 13th century BCE. He is often shown seated on a throne of coiled serpents.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/napirisha.jpg" alt="Napirisha" width="320" height="336" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: dynamosquito · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kurangun_central_panel,_male_and_female_deities_(Inshushinak_and_Napirisha).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
